          </div>

          <div class="story-copy">
            <p class="story-counter" data-story-counter>01 / 05</p>
            <p class="story-kicker" data-story-kicker>Research trajectory</p>
            <h1 class="story-title" data-story-title>Fernando Miramontes Forattini</h1>
            <p class="story-body" data-story-copy>
              I study how power, institutions, classifications, and digital technologies shape governance, accountability, and vulnerability.
            </p>
            <div class="story-actions" data-story-actions>
              <a class="button" href="#research" data-story-primary>Explore Research</a>
              <a class="button secondary" href="#contact" data-story-secondary>Contact</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="scroll-hint">scroll ↓</p>
          <a class="skip-link" href="#agenda">Skip</a>
        </div>

        <div class="story-steps" aria-hidden="true">
          <div
            data-story-step
            data-kicker="Research trajectory"
            data-title="Fernando Miramontes Forattini"
            data-copy="I study how power, institutions, classifications, and digital technologies shape governance, accountability, and vulnerability."
            data-primary-label="Explore Research"
            data-primary-href="#research"
            data-secondary-label="Contact"
            data-secondary-href="#contact"
          ></div>
          <div
            data-story-step
            data-kicker="Historical sociology"
            data-title="Institutions leave traces"
            data-copy="My work begins with archives, reforms, political conflict, and the long institutional histories that shape public life."
          ></div>
          <div
            data-story-step
            data-kicker="Governance and inequality"
            data-title="Power becomes practice"
            data-copy="Across corruption, classification, race, gender, and public services, I examine how authority is organized and how vulnerability is produced."
          ></div>
          <div
            data-story-step
            data-kicker="Sociotechnical systems"
            data-title="Technology enters the field"
            data-copy="My current research connects digital governance, AI, care ethics, and institutional analysis to examine how technical systems reshape public accountability."
          ></div>
          <div
            data-story-step
            data-kicker="Academic agenda"
            data-title="One line of inquiry, many sites"
            data-copy="The throughline is a research agenda on power, governance, inequality, and technology as connected social and institutional problems."
            data-primary-label="View Publications"
            data-primary-href="#publications"
            data-secondary-label="CV"
            data-secondary-href="#cv"
          ></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="content">
        <section class="panel" id="agenda" aria-labelledby="agenda-title">
          <div class="panel-inner">
            <div>
              <p class="section-kicker">Agenda</p>
              <h2 class="section-title" id="agenda-title">Power, institutions, technologies.</h2>
              <p class="section-copy">
                I am a historical sociologist and interdisciplinary social scientist. My research
                examines how institutions, norms, classifications, and technical systems organize
                authority, produce vulnerabilities, and shape contemporary forms of governance.
              </p>
              <div class="prestige-strip" aria-label="Academic affiliations and venues">
                <span>MSCA Fellow</span>
                <span>Dublin City University</span>
                <span>Research Ireland</span>
                <span>University of Chicago</span>
                <span>Cambridge University Press</span>
                <span>Oxford University Press</span>
                <span>MIT Press</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="agenda-line" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg class="mini-line" viewBox="0 0 640 520">
                <path d="M42 420 C112 354 178 326 240 336 C316 348 336 424 414 396 C494 368 500 258 426 228 C342 194 248 264 202 184 C162 112 238 56 326 98 C428 146 408 238 502 238 C568 238 594 188 566 142" />
                <rect x="86" y="252" width="92" height="116" rx="2" />
                <path d="M86 252 l46 -44 l46 44" />
                <circle cx="304" cy="178" r="52" />
                <path d="M266 214 c24 28 74 30 104 4" />
                <circle cx="466" cy="330" r="26" />
                <circle cx="526" cy="278" r="18" />
                <circle cx="558" cy="354" r="20" />
                <path d="M486 316 l24 -26" />
                <path d="M490 338 l48 12" />
              </svg>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel dark" id="research" aria-labelledby="research-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Research</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="research-title">Three clusters, one problem.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              The work moves across historical sociology, governance, inequality, and information systems,
              but the analytic question remains consistent: how do social orders become durable, contested,
              and technologically mediated?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="cluster-grid">
            <article class="cluster-card">
              <span class="cluster-number">01</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Governance, corruption, and institutional power</h3>
                <p>
                  Anti-corruption reforms, public services, state capacity, opacity, institutional change,
                  and the historical sociology of governance.
                </p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="cluster-card">
              <span class="cluster-number">02</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Inequality, classification, and recognition</h3>
                <p>
                  Race, Indigeneity, identity categories, social vulnerability, environmental destruction,
                  and how classification systems shape political life.
                </p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="cluster-card">
              <span class="cluster-number">03</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Technology, AI, and sociotechnical governance</h3>
                <p>
                  Digital public services, responsible AI, care ethics, gendered harm, information systems,
                  and institutional conditions for technical change.
                </p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="publications" aria-labelledby="publications-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Publications</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="publications-title">Publications as research architecture.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              A selected set of publications and forthcoming work. The full site can later turn this into
              a filterable publications archive with DOI, BibTeX, and downloadable PDFs.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="work-grid">
            <article class="work-card featured">
              <div>
                <p class="meta">American Behavioral Scientist · 2026</p>
                <h3>Beyond “best practices”: A historical-sociological critique of privatization, decentralization, and anti-corruption reforms in 1990s Brazil.</h3>
              </div>
              <p>
                A bridge between historical sociology, governance reform, and institutional critique.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="work-card">
              <p class="meta">MIT Press · 2026</p>
              <h3>The rise and evolution of anti-corruption impulses in the United States and Brazil.</h3>
              <div class="tag-row">
                <span class="tag">Governance</span>
                <span class="tag">Historical Sociology</span>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="work-card">
              <p class="meta">Oxford University Press · 2026</p>
              <h3>The weight of the “pardo” category: Official statistics and the deracialization of Indigenous peoples.</h3>
              <div class="tag-row">
                <span class="tag">Classification</span>
                <span class="tag">Inequality</span>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="work-card">
              <p class="meta">ACM SIGMIS Database · 2025</p>
              <h3>Breaking boundaries: Advancing gender and technology research to combat sextortion.</h3>
              <div class="tag-row">
                <span class="tag">Technology</span>
                <span class="tag">Gender</span>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="work-card">
              <p class="meta">Information Systems Management · 2026</p>
              <h3>Prometheus ambition, Zeusian order: Why leaps in information systems require structurally aware ethical frameworks.</h3>
              <div class="tag-row">
                <span class="tag">AI Ethics</span>
                <span class="tag">Institutions</span>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel dark" id="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Projects</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="projects-title">From scholarship to public systems.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              The public-facing work connects sociological analysis to policy, digital governance,
              responsible innovation, and institutions concerned with inequality and accountability.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="project-grid">
            <article class="project-card">
              <p class="meta">Current agenda</p>
              <h3>Digital governance and safer public services</h3>
              <p>
                Research on sexual corruption, public service encounters, gendered vulnerability,
                and the design of technological systems that do not reproduce institutional harm.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article class="project-card">
              <p class="meta">AI and auditability</p>
              <h3>AI agents in gender-sensitive public service encounters</h3>
              <p>
                Work on auditing AI agents through sociological, ethical, and institutional lenses,
                with attention to stronger configurations, care, and evidence traceability.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article class="project-card">
              <p class="meta">Policy and civil society</p>
              <h3>SDGs, governance, and public accountability</h3>
              <p>
                Engagement with ODS 9 and 16, anti-corruption policy, digital governance,
                and applied institutional analysis.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article class="project-card">
              <p class="meta">Public voice</p>
              <h3>Media, essays, and invited talks</h3>
              <p>
                Commentary and invited lectures on corruption, technology, inequality, AI,
                governance, and public policy across academic and public venues.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="teaching" aria-labelledby="teaching-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Teaching</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="teaching-title">Historical depth, technical literacy.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              Teaching spans historical sociology, theory, governance, inequality, digital transformation,
              AI, sustainable innovation, and research methods in the social sciences.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="teaching-grid">
            <article class="teaching-card">
              <h3>Digital transformation and sustainable change</h3>
              <p>Governance, organizational change, institutional trust, and social impacts of technology.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="teaching-card">
              <h3>AI and sustainable innovation</h3>
              <p>Artificial intelligence, inequality, institutional conditions, and responsible innovation.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="teaching-card">
              <h3>Historical sociology and methods</h3>
              <p>Comparative historical analysis, qualitative coding, institutional analysis, and social data.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="cv" aria-labelledby="cv-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">CV</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="cv-title">Academic profile at a glance.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              A concise version of the curriculum vitae, organized around current appointment,
              training, editorial work, and selected areas of teaching and research.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="cv-grid">
            <article class="cv-card">
              <p class="meta">Current</p>
              <h3>Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellow</h3>
              <p>Dublin City University / Research Ireland, 2024-2026.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="cv-card">
              <p class="meta">Education</p>
              <h3>PhD in Social History</h3>
              <p>Pontifícia Universidade Católica de São Paulo, with visiting doctoral research at the University of Chicago.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="cv-card">
              <p class="meta">Editorial</p>
              <h3>Senior Editor</h3>
              <p>Information Systems Management, Taylor & Francis, 2025-present.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="cv-card">
              <p class="meta">Fields</p>
              <h3>Historical sociology, governance, inequality, and technology</h3>
              <p>Research and teaching across institutional analysis, digital governance, AI, classification, and methods.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel contact-panel" id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
          <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Contact</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="contact-title">For research, teaching, and collaboration.</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="contact-grid">
            <article class="contact-card">
              <h3>Fernando Miramontes Forattini, PhD</h3>
              <p>
                Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellow<br />
                Dublin City University / Research Ireland
              </p>
            </article>
            <article class="contact-card">
              <h3>Links</h3>
              <div class="link-list">
                <a href="mailto:fernandomforattini@gmail.com">Email <span>↗</span></a>
                <a href="#" aria-disabled="true">Google Scholar <span>soon</span></a>
                <a href="#" aria-disabled="true">ORCID <span>soon</span></a>
                <a href="#" aria-disabled="true">LinkedIn <span>soon</span></a>
                <a href="#cv">CV <span>view</span></a>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <span>© 2026 Fernando Miramontes Forattini</span>
      <span>fernandomforattini.com</span>
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "../scripts/scroll-story.js";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
